- **Convert messages first:** `useChat` sends `UIMessage[]`. Run them through `convertToModelMessages` (it's async, so `await` it) before passing them to `streamText`.
- **Custom endpoint:** `useChat()` posts to `/api/chat` by default. For another URL, use `useChat({ transport: new DefaultChatTransport({ api: '/api/other' }) })`, with `DefaultChatTransport` imported from `ai`.
- **Rendering:** `useChat` no longer manages `input` or `handleSubmit`. Keep the input in your own state and call `sendMessage`. Render `message.parts` rather than `message.content`.
- **Older SDK versions:** The docs I found only show the `createUIMessageStreamResponse` form. In earlier v5-era releases, the equivalent was `return result.toUIMessageStreamResponse()`. If your installed `ai` package doesn't export `toUIMessageStream`, use that instead.

`onFinish` is the one in AI SDK 6 and earlier. In AI SDK 7 (the current version), the callback is renamed `onEnd`. `onFinish` still works there as a deprecated alias. Both run once, after the last step. `onStepFinish` is the per-step callback, so don't use it for this.

```ts
import { streamText, isStepCount } from 'ai';

const result = streamText({
  model: 'anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5',
  prompt: 'What is the weather in San Francisco?',
  tools,                       // your tools
  stopWhen: isStepCount(5),    // multi-step

  // Runs once, after ALL steps are done (AI SDK 7)
  async onEnd({ text, totalUsage, finishReason }) {
    await db.messages.insert({
      text,                                  // text of the final step
      inputTokens: totalUsage.inputTokens,   // summed across all steps
      outputTokens: totalUsage.outputTokens,
      totalTokens: totalUsage.totalTokens,
      finishReason,
    });
  },
});

return result.toUIMessageStreamResponse();
```

- **AI SDK 6 or earlier:** rename `onEnd` to `onFinish`. The event shape is the same for this use case.
- **`totalUsage` vs `usage`:** in v6's `onFinish`, `usage` covers only the last step and `totalUsage` covers all steps. Use `totalUsage` to bill the whole run. The v7 type tests also keep `totalUsage` on the `onEnd` event.
- **`text`:** this is the final step's text only. If earlier steps produced text before tool calls and you want all of it, build it from `steps` or `content`.
- **Aborted streams:** if the client aborts, for example with `useChat`'s `stop()`, these callbacks may not fire. A community report from 2024 describes this, and I haven't checked whether it's fixed. If you need to save aborted runs, test that case.
- **Keep it fast:** errors thrown in callbacks are caught and swallowed, and slow work delays the lifecycle. For heavy work, enqueue it instead.

Use **`onEnd`**. In AI SDK 7 it replaces `onFinish`, which still works as a deprecated alias. `onEnd` runs once, after all steps have finished.

```ts
import { streamText, isStepCount } from 'ai';

const result = streamText({
  model: yourModel,
  prompt: 'Use a tool, then summarize the result.',
  tools,
  stopWhen: isStepCount(5),

  async onEnd({ text, usage, finishReason }) {
    // text  -> text from the final step
    // usage -> token usage summed across ALL steps
    await db.messages.insert({
      text,
      inputTokens: usage.inputTokens,
      outputTokens: usage.outputTokens,
      totalTokens: usage.totalTokens,
      finishReason,
    });
  },
});

return result.toUIMessageStreamResponse();
```

- **Text:** `event.text` is the text from the final step only. This is usually the final answer after any tool calls. If you need the content from every step, use `event.content` or `event.steps`.
- **Usage:** `event.usage` is aggregated across all steps. `event.finalStep.usage` gives the final step's usage alone.
- **Per-step callback:** `onStepEnd` fires after each step, so it isn't the right place for the final save. It replaces the deprecated `onStepFinish`.
- **Older code:** In v6 and earlier, `onFinish`'s `usage` covered only the final step. You had to read `totalUsage` for the sum. `totalUsage` is deprecated in v7.
- **Consuming the stream:** The callback only fires once the stream is consumed. Returning the stream response to the client does this. If you aren't streaming to a client, call `result.consumeStream()`.

Use `result.fullStream`, which yields every event as a discriminated union on `type` (AI SDK v5):

```ts
import { streamText, tool, stepCountIs } from 'ai';
import { openai } from '@ai-sdk/openai';
import { z } from 'zod';

const result = streamText({
  model: openai('gpt-4o'),
  prompt: 'What is the weather in Paris?',
  tools: {
    getWeather: tool({
      description: 'Get the weather for a city',
      inputSchema: z.object({ city: z.string() }),
      execute: async ({ city }) => ({ city, tempC: 18 }),
    }),
  },
  stopWhen: stepCountIs(5), // allow the model to continue after tool results
});

for await (const part of result.fullStream) {
  switch (part.type) {
    case 'text-delta':
      process.stdout.write(part.text);
      break;
    case 'tool-call':
      console.log('\n[tool-call]', part.toolName, part.input);
      break;
    case 'tool-result':
      console.log('[tool-result]', part.toolName, part.output);
      break;
    case 'tool-error':
      console.error('[tool-error]', part.toolName, part.error);
      break;
    case 'error':
      console.error('[error]', part.error);
      break;
    case 'finish':
      console.log('\n[finish]', part.finishReason, part.totalUsage);
      break;
    // also available: 'start', 'start-step', 'finish-step',
    // 'text-start', 'text-end', 'reasoning-start/delta/end',
    // 'tool-input-start/delta/end', 'source', 'file', 'abort', 'raw'
  }
}
```

Notes:
- `result.textStream` gives only text; `fullStream` gives everything.
- In v4 the fields were named differently: `textDelta`, `args`, `result`.

In the current API (AI SDK 7), iterate over `result.stream`. It was called `result.fullStream` in v6. That name still works as a deprecated alias.

```ts
import { streamText } from 'ai';

const result = streamText({
  model: 'openai/gpt-5.4-nano',
  prompt: 'What is the weather in San Francisco?',
  tools: { /* your tools */ },
});

for await (const part of result.stream) {
  switch (part.type) {
    case 'text-delta':
      process.stdout.write(part.text);
      break;
    case 'tool-call':
      console.log('\nTool call:', part.toolName, part.input);
      break;
    case 'tool-result':
      console.log('\nTool result:', part.output);
      break;
    case 'tool-error':
      console.error('\nTool error:', part.error);
      break;
    case 'error':
      console.error('Stream error:', part.error);
      break;
  }
}
```

- `part.type` includes more than text and tools. The other types are `start`, `start-step`, `text-start`, `text-end`, `reasoning-delta`, `tool-input-start`, `tool-input-delta`, `finish-step`, `finish` and `abort`. Handle only the ones you need.
- Text deltas are on `part.text`. In AI SDK 5 and later they are no longer on `textDelta`.
- I took the `tool-call` and `tool-result` case labels and `part.text` from the docs. I didn't find the exact field names for `part.toolName`, `part.input`, `part.output` and `part.error` in the results, so check them against the types if you rely on them.
- If you're still on v6, replace `result.stream` with `result.fullStream`.

Iterate over `result.fullStream` and switch on `part.type`:

```ts
import { streamText, tool, stepCountIs } from 'ai';
import { z } from 'zod';

const result = streamText({
  model: 'anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.5', // any model string or provider model
  prompt: 'What is the weather in San Francisco?',
  tools: {
    weather: tool({
      description: 'Get the weather in a location',
      inputSchema: z.object({ location: z.string() }),
      execute: async ({ location }) => ({ location, temperature: 72 }),
    }),
  },
  stopWhen: stepCountIs(5), // lets the model respond after the tool result
});

for await (const part of result.fullStream) {
  switch (part.type) {
    case 'text-delta':
      process.stdout.write(part.text);
      break;
    case 'tool-call':
      console.log('\nTool call:', part.toolName, part.input);
      break;
    case 'tool-result':
      console.log('Tool result:', part.toolName, part.output);
      break;
    case 'tool-error':
      console.error('Tool error:', part.toolName, part.error);
      break;
    case 'error':
      console.error('Stream error:', part.error);
      break;
    case 'finish':
      console.log('\nFinish:', part.finishReason);
      break;
  }
}
```

- **Field names (v5 and later):**
  - Text deltas use `part.text`. In v4 this was `part.textDelta`.
  - Tool calls use `part.input`, which was `args` in v4.
  - Tool results use `part.output`, which was `result` in v4.
- **Other event types:** `fullStream` also emits `start`, `start-step`, `text-start`, `text-end`, `reasoning-*`, `source`, `finish-step`, `abort` and others. Add cases for the ones you need.
- **Errors:** `error` and `tool-error` arrive as stream parts and aren't thrown. Handle them in the `switch`, and also wrap the loop in `try/catch` for errors outside the stream.
- **Multi-step tool use:** without `stopWhen`, the default is a single step. The loop will then end after the tool result, with no follow-up text from the model.
- **Text only:** if you only want text, iterate `result.textStream` instead.

Iterate over `result.fullStream`. It yields every event as a typed part, and you branch on `part.type`:

```ts
import { streamText, tool, stepCountIs } from 'ai';
import { z } from 'zod';

const result = streamText({
  model: 'anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.5', // or e.g. openai('gpt-4o')
  prompt: 'What is the weather in Berlin?',
  tools: {
    weather: tool({
      description: 'Get the weather for a city',
      inputSchema: z.object({ city: z.string() }),
      execute: async ({ city }) => ({ city, tempC: 21 }),
    }),
  },
  stopWhen: stepCountIs(5), // lets the model respond after the tool result
});

for await (const part of result.fullStream) {
  switch (part.type) {
    case 'text-delta':
      process.stdout.write(part.text);
      break;
    case 'tool-call':
      console.log('\nTool call:', part.toolName, part.input);
      break;
    case 'tool-result':
      console.log('Tool result:', part.toolName, part.output);
      break;
    case 'tool-error':
      console.error('Tool error:', part.toolName, part.error);
      break;
    case 'error':
      console.error('Stream error:', part.error);
      break;
    case 'finish':
      console.log('\nFinish:', part.finishReason, part.totalUsage);
      break;
  }
}
```

- **Field names (v5 and later):** `text-delta` uses `part.text`, not the v4 `textDelta`. `tool-call` uses `part.input`, not `args`. `tool-result` uses `part.output`, not `result`.
- **Other event types:** `fullStream` also emits `start`, `start-step`, `finish-step`, `text-start`, `text-end`, `reasoning-*`, `tool-input-start`, `tool-input-delta` and `source`. Add cases for any of these you need.
- **Multi-step runs:** the tool call and result only appear in a single stream if you set `stopWhen`. The default is `stepCountIs(1)`.
- **Errors:** errors show up as `error` parts in the stream instead of being thrown. Handle them inside the loop.
- **Text only:** `result.textStream` gives just the text chunks.

Sources: AI SDK docs on streaming and the `fullStream` custom-stream-format cookbook.

Use `McpServer.registerTool` with a [Zod](https://zod.dev) schema:

```ts
import { McpServer } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/mcp.js";
import { StdioServerTransport } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/stdio.js";
import { z } from "zod";

const server = new McpServer({ name: "demo-server", version: "1.0.0" });

server.registerTool(
  "add",
  {
    title: "Add Numbers",
    description: "Add two numbers together",
    inputSchema: { a: z.number(), b: z.number() }, // raw Zod shape, not z.object()
  },
  async ({ a, b }) => ({
    content: [{ type: "text", text: String(a + b) }],
  })
);

const transport = new StdioServerTransport();
await server.connect(transport);
```

Notes:
- In the v1.x SDK, `inputSchema` is a plain object of Zod validators (a "raw shape"). The SDK wraps it and validates inputs, so the handler's arguments are fully typed.
- You can optionally add an `outputSchema` (also a raw shape) and return `structuredContent` alongside `content`.
- Install with `npm install @modelcontextprotocol/sdk zod`.

Use `McpServer.registerTool` with a Zod schema (`@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` v1.x):

```ts
import { McpServer } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/mcp.js";
import { StdioServerTransport } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/stdio.js";
import { z } from "zod";

const server = new McpServer({
  name: "demo-server",
  version: "1.0.0",
});

server.registerTool(
  "add",
  {
    title: "Add Numbers",
    description: "Add two numbers together",
    inputSchema: {
      a: z.number().describe("First number"),
      b: z.number().describe("Second number"),
    },
  },
  async ({ a, b }) => ({
    content: [{ type: "text", text: String(a + b) }],
  })
);

const transport = new StdioServerTransport();
await server.connect(transport);
```

Notes:
- `inputSchema` is a plain object of Zod types (a "raw shape"), not `z.object(...)`. The SDK wraps it and converts it to JSON Schema for clients.
- Arguments are validated before your handler runs, and the handler's parameters are fully typed.
- Optionally, add an `outputSchema` and return `structuredContent` alongside `content` for structured results.
- To report a tool failure, return `{ content: [...], isError: true }`.

Use `McpServer.registerTool` and pass a Zod object (or any Standard Schema) as `inputSchema`. The SDK derives the JSON Schema, validates incoming arguments, and infers the handler's argument types.

```typescript
import { McpServer } from "@modelcontextprotocol/server";
import { StdioServerTransport } from "@modelcontextprotocol/server/stdio";
import * as z from "zod/v4";

const server = new McpServer({ name: "greeting-server", version: "1.0.0" });

server.registerTool(
  "greet",
  {
    description: "Greet someone by name",
    inputSchema: z.object({
      name: z.string().describe("Name of the person to greet"),
      times: z.number().int().max(5).optional(),
    }),
  },
  async ({ name, times }) => ({
    content: [
      {
        type: "text",
        text: Array.from({ length: times ?? 1 }, () => `Hello, ${name}!`).join("\n"),
      },
    ],
  }),
);

await server.connect(new StdioServerTransport());
```

- **Schema:** `inputSchema` is a `z.object({...})`, not a raw shape. Use `.describe()` on fields to give the model hints.
- **Validation:** Arguments are checked before your handler runs. Invalid calls are rejected and the handler never executes.
- **Return value:** The handler returns `{ content: [...] }`. You can also add an `outputSchema` and return `structuredContent`.
- **Other schema libraries:** ArkType and other Standard Schema libraries work the same way.
- **Low-level option:** If you need full control, the low-level `Server` class lets you write JSON Schema by hand in a `tools/list` handler.

Use `registerTool` with a Zod `inputSchema`. This is the current API in the v2 SDK, `@modelcontextprotocol/server`.

```bash
npm install @modelcontextprotocol/server zod
```

```ts
import { McpServer } from '@modelcontextprotocol/server';
import * as z from 'zod/v4';

const server = new McpServer({ name: 'catalog', version: '1.0.0' });

server.registerTool(
    'search',
    {
        description: 'Search the product catalog',
        inputSchema: z.object({
            query: z.string().describe('Substring to match against product names'),
            limit: z.number().int().max(50).optional()
        })
    },
    async ({ query, limit }) => {
        const results = ['Travel mug', 'Mug rack']; // your lookup here
        return { content: [{ type: 'text', text: results.slice(0, limit ?? 10).join('\n') }] };
    }
);
```

- **Schema:** The SDK turns the Zod schema into the JSON Schema that clients see. It validates arguments before your handler runs, and it infers the handler's argument types.
- **Invalid arguments:** The SDK returns a result with `isError: true` and a validation message. Your handler never runs.
- **Descriptions:** `.describe()` carries through to the JSON Schema. It's the only documentation the model gets for each argument.
- **Output:** You can add an optional `outputSchema` and return `structuredContent` alongside `content`.

**Version note:** In the older 1.x SDK (`@modelcontextprotocol/sdk`), `registerTool` takes a raw shape instead: `inputSchema: { query: z.string() }`. The `server.tool()` method is deprecated. For v2, you can migrate with `npx @modelcontextprotocol/codemod@latest v1-to-v2 .`.

Source: https://ts.sdk.modelcontextprotocol.io/v2/servers/tools.html

Use `server.registerTool(name, config, handler)` on an `McpServer`. Put a Zod object schema in `config.inputSchema`:

```typescript
import { McpServer } from "@modelcontextprotocol/server";
import { StdioServerTransport } from "@modelcontextprotocol/server/stdio";
import * as z from "zod/v4";

const server = new McpServer({ name: "catalog", version: "1.0.0" });

server.registerTool(
  "search",
  {
    description: "Search the product catalog",
    inputSchema: z.object({
      query: z.string().describe("Substring to match against product names"),
      limit: z.number().int().max(50).optional(),
    }),
  },
  async ({ query, limit }) => {
    const names = ["Espresso cup", "Travel mug", "Mug rack"]
      .filter(n => n.toLowerCase().includes(query.toLowerCase()))
      .slice(0, limit ?? 10);
    return { content: [{ type: "text", text: names.join("\n") }] };
  }
);

await server.connect(new StdioServerTransport());
```

- The SDK uses the one Zod schema to generate the JSON Schema it advertises to clients. It also validates incoming arguments and types the handler's parameters.
- If arguments fail validation, the client gets an `isError: true` result and the handler never runs.
- To report a failure the model can recover from, return `{ content: [...], isError: true }` from the handler.
- Optionally, add `outputSchema` and return `structuredContent` for machine-readable results. `title` and `annotations` are also available.
- The current packages are `@modelcontextprotocol/server` and Zod v4 (`zod/v4`). The package is ESM-only and needs Node 20 or later.

Use the **Streamable HTTP** transport, which replaces the old SSE transport. Here is a minimal stateless setup with Express:

```bash
npm i @modelcontextprotocol/sdk express zod
```

```ts
import express from "express";
import { z } from "zod";
import { McpServer } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/mcp.js";
import { StreamableHTTPServerTransport } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/streamableHttp.js";

function createServer() {
  const server = new McpServer({ name: "my-server", version: "1.0.0" });

  server.registerTool(
    "add",
    {
      title: "Add",
      description: "Add two numbers",
      inputSchema: { a: z.number(), b: z.number() },
    },
    async ({ a, b }) => ({
      content: [{ type: "text", text: String(a + b) }],
    })
  );

  return server;
}

const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

app.post("/mcp", async (req, res) => {
  const server = createServer();
  const transport = new StreamableHTTPServerTransport({
    sessionIdGenerator: undefined, // stateless
  });

  res.on("close", () => {
    transport.close();
    server.close();
  });

  await server.connect(transport);
  await transport.handleRequest(req, res, req.body);
});

// Stateless mode doesn't support server-initiated streams or session teardown
app.get("/mcp", (_req, res) => res.status(405).end());
app.delete("/mcp", (_req, res) => res.status(405).end());

app.listen(3000, () => console.log("MCP server on http://localhost:3000/mcp"));
```

Clients connect to `http://your-host:3000/mcp`. You can test it with:

```bash
npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector
```

**Notes:**
- **Stateful sessions:** If you need sessions (for resumability or server-to-client notifications), pass `sessionIdGenerator: () => randomUUID()`. Keep a map of transports keyed by the `mcp-session-id` header, and handle GET and DELETE as well as POST.
- **Production:** Put it behind HTTPS, add authentication (e.g. OAuth or bearer tokens), and validate the `Origin` and `Host` headers to guard against DNS rebinding. If you're binding locally, bind to `127.0.0.1`.

