Source: the [MCP TypeScript SDK Tools guide](https://ts.sdk.modelcontextprotocol.io/v2/servers/tools).

Use the Streamable HTTP transport. The SSE transport is deprecated. The current TypeScript SDK packages are `@modelcontextprotocol/server`, `@modelcontextprotocol/node`, and `@modelcontextprotocol/express`. This example is stateless, so each request gets its own server and transport:

```ts
import { createMcpExpressApp } from '@modelcontextprotocol/express';
import { NodeStreamableHTTPServerTransport } from '@modelcontextprotocol/node';
import { McpServer } from '@modelcontextprotocol/server';

const app = createMcpExpressApp();

app.post('/mcp', async (req, res) => {
    const server = new McpServer({ name: 'my-server', version: '1.0.0' });
    // register your tools/resources/prompts on `server` here

    const transport = new NodeStreamableHTTPServerTransport({
        sessionIdGenerator: undefined // stateless
    });
    await server.connect(transport);
    await transport.handleRequest(req, res, req.body);
});

app.listen(3000, () => console.log('MCP server listening on http://localhost:3000/mcp'));
```

Remote clients then connect to `http://your-host:3000/mcp`.

- **Stateful sessions:** If you need sessions, pass `sessionIdGenerator: () => randomUUID()`. Keep one transport per session in a map keyed by the `mcp-session-id` header. Create a new transport only when the request is an `initialize` request. Also route `GET` and `DELETE` on the same path to `transport.handleRequest(req, res)`. The docs' authorization tutorial has a full example of this pattern. It uses the older `StreamableHTTPServerTransport` name and imports.
- **Security:** For a remote deployment, put authentication middleware in front of the route and serve over HTTPS.
- **Package versions:** The docs I found use two sets of package names and imports. The `@modelcontextprotocol/*` split packages are in the SDK's current READMEs. The tutorial uses the older single-package names. Check which version of the SDK you have installed.

Use the Streamable HTTP transport. In the current TypeScript SDK, the Node and Express pieces are in separate packages: `@modelcontextprotocol/server`, `@modelcontextprotocol/node` and `@modelcontextprotocol/express`.

```ts
import { createMcpExpressApp } from '@modelcontextprotocol/express';
import { NodeStreamableHTTPServerTransport } from '@modelcontextprotocol/node';
import { McpServer } from '@modelcontextprotocol/server';

const app = createMcpExpressApp();

app.post('/mcp', async (req, res) => {
    // Stateless: a new server and transport for each request
    const server = new McpServer({ name: 'my-server', version: '1.0.0' });
    // register your tools/resources/prompts on `server` here

    const transport = new NodeStreamableHTTPServerTransport({
        sessionIdGenerator: undefined
    });

    await server.connect(transport);
    // Pass req.body because Express has already parsed it
    await transport.handleRequest(req, res, req.body);
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
    console.log('MCP server listening on http://localhost:3000/mcp');
});
```

Remote clients then connect to `http://<your-host>:3000/mcp`.

- **Stateless vs. stateful:** The example above is stateless, because `sessionIdGenerator: undefined` turns sessions off. If you want sessions, pass `sessionIdGenerator: () => randomUUID()` and keep one transport per session ID. Look up the transport using the `mcp-session-id` header. Also route `GET` and `DELETE` on the same path to `transport.handleRequest`.
- **Spec direction:** The 2026-07-28 spec revision makes the protocol stateless. Each request is a separate POST that carries its own protocol version and capabilities. The server replies with either JSON or an SSE stream.
- **Production:** Put the server behind HTTPS. Add authentication middleware, such as a bearer-token check on the `/mcp` route, before you expose it publicly.

I didn't confirm whether `createMcpExpressApp()` applies any default host or DNS-rebinding protection. Check the `@modelcontextprotocol/express` README for its options before you bind to a public interface.

Use Streamable HTTP. In the current v2 SDK, `createMcpHandler` turns a server factory into an HTTP handler, and a small adapter mounts it on Express.

```bash
npm install @modelcontextprotocol/server @modelcontextprotocol/express @modelcontextprotocol/node express zod
```

```ts
import { createMcpExpressApp } from '@modelcontextprotocol/express';
import { toNodeHandler } from '@modelcontextprotocol/node';
import { createMcpHandler, McpServer } from '@modelcontextprotocol/server';
import * as z from 'zod/v4';

// The factory runs once per request, so each call gets a fresh McpServer.
const handler = createMcpHandler(() => {
    const server = new McpServer({ name: 'notes', version: '1.0.0' });
    server.registerTool(
        'add-note',
        { description: 'Append a note', inputSchema: z.object({ text: z.string() }) },
        async ({ text }) => ({ content: [{ type: 'text', text: `Saved: ${text}` }] })
    );
    return server;
});

// For remote access, bind to all interfaces and list the hostnames you serve.
const app = createMcpExpressApp({ host: '0.0.0.0', allowedHosts: ['api.example.com'] });
const node = toNodeHandler(handler);
app.all('/mcp', (req, res) => void node(req, res, req.body));

app.listen(3000, '0.0.0.0', () => console.log('MCP server on http://0.0.0.0:3000/mcp'));
```

Remote clients then connect to `https://api.example.com/mcp`.

- **DNS rebinding protection:** `createMcpExpressApp` validates the `Host` and `Origin` headers. With the default `127.0.0.1` bind, it rejects non-localhost values with a 403. Binding to `0.0.0.0` drops that default, so you have to set `allowedHosts` yourself, as above.
- **Stateless by default:** because the factory builds a new server per request, this scales horizontally with no session map. If you need sessions, resumability, or server-pushed notifications, use `NodeStreamableHTTPServerTransport` with `sessionIdGenerator: () => randomUUID()` and keep a map of transports by `Mcp-Session-Id`. The "Sessions, state, and scaling" page in the SDK docs covers this.
- **Auth:** for a public server, add `requireBearerAuth` from `@modelcontextprotocol/express`. The handler can read the result as `ctx.http.authInfo`.
- **On v1 (`@modelcontextprotocol/sdk`):** the equivalent is `StreamableHTTPServerTransport` from `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/streamableHttp.js`. You create a server and a transport inside `app.post('/mcp', ...)`, call `server.connect(transport)`, then call `transport.handleRequest(req, res, req.body)`. The old HTTP+SSE transport is deprecated.

Sources: the SDK's "Serve with Express" and "Sessions, state, and scaling" docs at ts.sdk.modelcontextprotocol.io/v2.

Use the SDK's Streamable HTTP transport. The current v2 SDK has a helper that handles most of the setup.

```bash
npm install @modelcontextprotocol/server @modelcontextprotocol/express @modelcontextprotocol/node express zod
```

```ts
import { createMcpExpressApp } from '@modelcontextprotocol/express';
import { toNodeHandler } from '@modelcontextprotocol/node';
import { createMcpHandler, McpServer } from '@modelcontextprotocol/server';
import * as z from 'zod/v4';

// The factory runs once per request, so the server is stateless and scales horizontally.
const handler = createMcpHandler(() => {
    const server = new McpServer({ name: 'notes', version: '1.0.0' });
    server.registerTool(
        'add-note',
        { description: 'Append a note', inputSchema: z.object({ text: z.string() }) },
        async ({ text }) => ({ content: [{ type: 'text', text: `Saved: ${text}` }] })
    );
    return server;
});

// For remote clients, bind to all interfaces and list the hostnames you serve.
const app = createMcpExpressApp({ host: '0.0.0.0', allowedHosts: ['api.example.com'] });
const node = toNodeHandler(handler);
app.all('/mcp', (req, res) => void node(req, res, req.body));

app.listen(3000);
```

Clients then connect to `https://api.example.com/mcp`.

- **DNS rebinding protection:** `createMcpExpressApp()` validates the `Host` and `Origin` headers. With the default `127.0.0.1` bind, it rejects non-localhost requests with a 403. If you bind to `0.0.0.0` for remote access, that default goes away, so set `allowedHosts` as shown.
- **Auth:** For a public server, add bearer auth with `requireBearerAuth`. The docs say `toNodeHandler` forwards the result, and tool handlers read it as `ctx.http.authInfo`.
- **Sessions:** `createMcpHandler` is stateless by default. If you need 2025-era sessions, use `NodeStreamableHTTPServerTransport` directly with `sessionIdGenerator: () => randomUUID()`. You then have to keep a map of transports by `Mcp-Session-Id`. Passing `sessionIdGenerator: undefined` gives stateless mode with the manual transport.
- **Manual stateless route:** This is the lower-level alternative to `createMcpHandler`:
  ```ts
  app.post('/mcp', async (req, res) => {
      const server = getServer(); // your own McpServer factory
      const transport = new NodeStreamableHTTPServerTransport({ sessionIdGenerator: undefined });
      await server.connect(transport);
      await transport.handleRequest(req, res, req.body);
  });
  ```
- **v1 SDK:** If you're still on `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` (v1), the equivalent class is `StreamableHTTPServerTransport`, imported from `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/streamableHttp.js`. The old HTTP+SSE transport is deprecated.

Sources: the v2 docs at ts.sdk.modelcontextprotocol.io/v2/serving/express.html and the SDK's `NodeStreamableHTTPServerTransport` API reference. I added `app.listen(3000)` myself because it's standard Express.

Use structured outputs: pass a JSON schema via `output_config.format`, and the response text will be valid JSON matching it.

```python
import json
import anthropic

client = anthropic.Anthropic()  # uses ANTHROPIC_API_KEY

schema = {
    "type": "object",
    "properties": {
        "name": {"type": "string"},
        "email": {"type": "string"},
        "plan": {"type": "string", "enum": ["free", "pro", "enterprise"]},
    },
    "required": ["name", "email", "plan"],
    "additionalProperties": False,  # required for structured outputs
}

response = client.messages.create(
    model="claude-sonnet-4-5",
    max_tokens=1024,
    messages=[
        {
            "role": "user",
            "content": "Extract the contact info: Jane Doe (jane@example.com) wants the pro plan.",
        }
    ],
    output_config={
        "format": {
            "type": "json_schema",
            "schema": schema,
        }
    },
)

data = json.loads(response.content[0].text)
print(data)  # {'name': 'Jane Doe', 'email': 'jane@example.com', 'plan': 'pro'}
```

Notes:
- Structured outputs are supported on recent models (e.g., Claude Sonnet 4.5, Opus 4.5, Haiku 4.5). Check the docs for your model.
- Only a subset of JSON Schema is supported (e.g., no recursive schemas, and numeric or string constraints are limited).
- Check `response.stop_reason`. If it's `"max_tokens"` or `"refusal"`, the output may be incomplete or not match the schema.
- On older SDK/API versions, this feature was in beta as `client.beta.messages.create(..., betas=["structured-outputs-2025-11-13"], output_format={...})`. Upgrade the SDK (`pip install -U anthropic`) to use the current form.
- If your model doesn't support structured outputs, you can define a tool whose `input_schema` is your schema and force it with `tool_choice={"type": "tool", "name": "..."}`. The JSON will be in the `tool_use` block's `input`.

