In Mastra v1, use `RequestContext`. It was called `RuntimeContext` in pre-v1 releases.

```ts
import { createTool } from "@mastra/core/tools";
import { RequestContext } from "@mastra/core/request-context";
import { z } from "zod";

// Tool reads the value from the request context
export const getOrdersTool = createTool({
  id: "get-orders",
  description: "Fetch the current user's orders",
  inputSchema: z.object({}),
  execute: async (_input, context) => {
    const userId = context?.requestContext?.get("user-id");
    return { orders: await db.orders.findMany({ where: { userId } }) };
  },
});

// Pass it per request when calling the agent
const requestContext = new RequestContext();
requestContext.set("user-id", "user_123");

const res = await agent.generate("What are my recent orders?", {
  requestContext,
});
```

When you use Mastra's server, set it in middleware instead of constructing it yourself:

```ts
export const mastra = new Mastra({
  agents: { myAgent },
  server: {
    middleware: [
      async (c, next) => {
        const requestContext = c.get("requestContext");
        requestContext.set("user-id", c.req.header("x-user-id"));
        await next();
      },
    ],
  },
});
```

On older versions (pre-v1), the equivalent is `RuntimeContext` from `@mastra/core/runtime-context`. You pass it as `{ runtimeContext }`, and tools read it via `execute: async ({ runtimeContext }) => runtimeContext.get("user-id")`.

