Things to know:
- **Tool names are prefixed with the server name by default in 2.x.** For example, `search` on the `docs` server becomes `docs__search`. Set `prefixToolNameWithServerName: false` to turn this off. If two servers expose the same tool name while prefixing is off, `listTools()` throws.
- **`listToolsets()` groups tools by server.** `listTools()` returns one flat list.
- **1.x API:** If you're on `@langchain/mcp-adapters` 1.x, the equivalent is `new MultiServerMCPClient({ math: {...}, weather: { transport: "http", url } })` followed by `await client.getTools()`. In 2.x that class and `getTools()` still work but are deprecated.
- **Servers you write yourself:** Build them with the MCP SDK. In 2.x, import SDK client types from `@modelcontextprotocol/client`.

Sources: the [LangChain.js MCP docs](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/javascript/langchain/mcp) and the [@langchain/mcp-adapters 2.0.0 release notes](https://github.com/langchain-ai/langchainjs/releases/tag/%40langchain/mcp-adapters%402.0.0).

Use `RequestContext` (called `RuntimeContext` before Mastra v1). Set values on it per request, pass it to the agent call, and read it in the tool's `execute`.

```ts
import { createTool } from "@mastra/core/tools";
import { RequestContext } from "@mastra/core/request-context";
import { z } from "zod";

// Tool: read the value
export const getOrders = createTool({
  id: "get-orders",
  description: "Fetch the current user's orders",
  inputSchema: z.object({}),
  execute: async (_input, { requestContext }) => {
    const userId = requestContext?.get("user-id");
    return await db.orders.findMany({ where: { userId } });
  },
});

// Call site: set the value per request
const requestContext = new RequestContext();
requestContext.set("user-id", "user_123");

const result = await agent.generate("What are my recent orders?", {
  requestContext,
});
```

**With the Mastra server**, set it in middleware so every agent and tool call gets it automatically:

```ts
export const mastra = new Mastra({
  agents: { agent },
  server: {
    middleware: [
      async (c, next) => {
        const user = await authenticate(c.req.header("Authorization"));
        c.get("requestContext").set("user-id", user.id);
        await next();
      },
    ],
  },
});
```

**On older versions (0.x):** import `RuntimeContext` from `@mastra/core/runtime-context`, pass `{ runtimeContext }` to `generate`, and in tools use `execute: async ({ context, runtimeContext }) => runtimeContext.get("user-id")`.

Use `RequestContext`. It was called `RuntimeContext` before Mastra v1. You set values on it, pass it to the agent call, and tools read it from their second `execute` argument.

```typescript
import { RequestContext } from '@mastra/core/request-context'
import { createTool } from '@mastra/core/tools'
import { z } from 'zod'

// 1. Tool reads the value from the execution context (2nd arg)
export const getOrdersTool = createTool({
  id: 'get-orders',
  description: "Fetch the current user's orders",
  inputSchema: z.object({ status: z.string().optional() }),
  requestContextSchema: z.object({ userId: z.string() }), // optional: validates + types the context
  execute: async ({ status }, context) => {
    const userId = context?.requestContext?.get('userId')
    return { orders: await db.orders.find({ userId, status }) }
  },
})

// 2. Set the value per request and pass it when calling the agent
const requestContext = new RequestContext<{ userId: string }>()
requestContext.set('userId', currentUser.id)

const agent = mastra.getAgent('supportAgent')
const result = await agent.generate("What's the status of my orders?", {
  requestContext,
})
```

- **The model never sees the value.** `userId` goes straight to your tool code and isn't part of the prompt or the tool's `inputSchema`. The model can't change it or invent it.
- **Setting it from the server:** If you run the Mastra server, populate the context in middleware, for example from an auth token. That way the value doesn't depend on client input.
- **Memory:** For memory scoping, the docs say a value set in middleware under the reserved key `MASTRA_RESOURCE_ID_KEY` takes precedence over client-provided values. This stops one user from reading another user's memory.
- **Agent configuration:** `instructions`, `tools` and `model` can also be functions of `({ requestContext })`, so the same context can change the agent's configuration per request.
- **Migrating:** If you're upgrading old code, replace `runtimeContext` with `requestContext`. `npx @mastra/codemod@latest v1/runtime-context .` does this automatically. Tool `execute` is now `(inputData, context)`, so use `context.requestContext` rather than destructuring it from the first argument.

Docs: https://mastra.ai/docs/server/request-context

