In SvelteKit, `src/lib` is aliased as **`$lib`**. It works in routes, components, and server files without relative paths.

```ts
// src/lib/utils.ts
export function formatDate(date: Date): string {
	return date.toLocaleDateString('en-US');
}
```

```svelte
<!-- src/routes/+page.svelte -->
<script lang="ts">
	import { formatDate } from '$lib/utils';

	const today = formatDate(new Date());
</script>

<p>Today is {today}</p>
```

It works the same way in a server file:

```ts
// src/routes/+page.server.ts
import { formatDate } from '$lib/utils';

export function load() {
	return { today: formatDate(new Date()) };
}
```

Notes:
- Omit the file extension (`$lib/utils`, not `$lib/utils.ts`).
- Anything in `src/lib/server/` can only be imported from server code, via `$lib/server/...`.
- You can add your own aliases in `svelte.config.js` under `kit.alias`, e.g. `alias: { $components: 'src/components' }`.

The alias is `$lib`. It maps to `src/lib`, so you can import from it in any route, component, or server file without relative paths.

```ts
// src/lib/utils.ts
export function formatDate(date: Date): string {
  return date.toLocaleDateString('en-US');
}
```

```svelte
<!-- src/routes/+page.svelte -->
<script lang="ts">
  import { formatDate } from '$lib/utils';

  const today = formatDate(new Date());
</script>

<p>{today}</p>
```

It works the same in a load function:

```ts
// src/routes/+page.ts
import { formatDate } from '$lib/utils';

export function load() {
  return { today: formatDate(new Date()) };
}
```

Notes:
- Omit the `.ts` extension (`$lib/utils`, not `$lib/utils.ts`).
- Files under `src/lib/server/` can only be imported from server code, using `$lib/server/...`.
- You can add more aliases via `kit.alias` in `svelte.config.js`.

In current versions of `@astrojs/cloudflare` (v13 and later, used with Astro 6 and later), you import `env` from `cloudflare:workers`. The old `Astro.locals.runtime.env` was removed and now throws an error.

**1. Declare the binding in `wrangler.jsonc`**

```jsonc
{
  "name": "my-astro-app",
  "kv_namespaces": [{ "binding": "MY_KV", "id": "<namespace_id>" }]
}
```

**2. Read it in a page**

```astro
---
// src/pages/index.astro
import { env } from "cloudflare:workers";

export const prerender = false; // the page must be rendered on demand

const value = await env.MY_KV.get("some-key");
---

<h1>{value ?? "not found"}</h1>
```

The same import works in API routes and middleware:

```ts
// src/pages/api/kv.ts
import type { APIRoute } from "astro";
import { env } from "cloudflare:workers";

export const GET: APIRoute = async () => {
  const value = await env.MY_KV.get("some-key");
  return new Response(value);
};
```

**Notes**
- **Migrating:** The old `Astro.locals.runtime.env` is replaced by `import { env } from "cloudflare:workers"`. The other old runtime properties moved too:
  - `runtime.cf` is now `Astro.request.cf`.
  - `runtime.ctx` is now `Astro.locals.cfContext`.
  - `runtime.caches` is now the global `caches` object.
- **Dev server:** `astro dev` now runs in workerd, so the KV binding also works locally. Local KV is simulated and starts empty. You can seed it, or use the adapter's `remoteBindings` option to reach real KV.
- **Prerendered pages:** Only read bindings in routes with `prerender = false`. Importing `cloudflare:workers` from prerendered pages or shared middleware has caused build errors in hybrid sites (withastro/astro#15237). I didn't confirm whether that issue is fixed in the latest release.
- **Types:** Run `npx wrangler types` to generate types for `env`, so `env.MY_KV` is typed as `KVNamespace`.

Source: the [@astrojs/cloudflare docs](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/cloudflare/) and the `@astrojs/cloudflare` v13 release notes.

In current SvelteKit (v3), the alias is **`#lib`**. SvelteKit no longer generates it for you, so you declare it in `package.json` using Node's subpath imports:

```json
// package.json
{
	"imports": {
		"#lib": "./src/lib/index.js",
		"#lib/*": "./src/lib/*"
	}
}
```

Then import your helper in a route. You must include the file extension:

```svelte
<!-- src/routes/+page.svelte -->
<script lang="ts">
	import { myHelper } from '#lib/utils.js';

	const result = myHelper();
</script>

<p>{result}</p>
```

The same import works in `+page.ts`, `+server.ts` and other route files.

- **Extension:** The migration docs say to add the module extension (`.js` or `.ts`). I used `.js` because that's what the docs' example uses, and TypeScript normally resolves it to `utils.ts`. I didn't check whether `#lib/utils.ts` also works. It may need `allowImportingTsExtensions` in your tsconfig.
- **Older projects:** On SvelteKit 2 and earlier, the alias was `$lib`, and SvelteKit configured it automatically. You would write `import { myHelper } from '$lib/utils'`. If you're migrating to v3, replace `$lib` with `#lib` and add the extensions.

Set `changeDetection` to `ChangeDetectionStrategy.Default`, which checks the component on every change detection run:

```ts
import { Component, ChangeDetectionStrategy } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-example',
  template: `{{ value }}`,
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Default,
})
export class ExampleComponent {
  value = 0;
}
```

In the newest Angular versions, where OnPush is becoming the default for new components, `ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager` is the new name for the same behavior. If your version has it, you can use it instead:

```ts
changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager,
```

If you only need a one-off check from a component that is OnPush, inject `ChangeDetectorRef` and call `markForCheck()`. Better still, drive the template from signals, which mark the component dirty automatically.

Set `changeDetection` to `ChangeDetectionStrategy.Default` in the component decorator. Omitting it has the same effect, but being explicit makes the intent clear.

```ts
import { ChangeDetectionStrategy, Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-example',
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Default, // checked on every CD run
  template: `<p>{{ value }}</p>`,
})
export class ExampleComponent {
  value = 'Hello';
}
```

Notes:
- A component with `Default` is checked on every change detection pass, regardless of whether its inputs changed.
- If a parent is `OnPush` and not marked dirty, the parent's subtree (including your `Default` child) is skipped. A `Default` component only runs on every pass if its ancestors are also being checked.
- In newer Angular versions where OnPush is becoming the default for new projects, keep the explicit `changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Default` so the component keeps this behavior. Newer releases may also expose it under a new name (`Eager`), so check the docs for your version.

In current SvelteKit the alias is **`#lib`**. It replaced the older `$lib` alias, and `$lib` still appears in a lot of existing code and tutorials.

`#lib` is a Node subpath import. `sv create` adds it to your `package.json`:

```json
{
  "imports": {
    "#lib": "./src/lib/index.js",
    "#lib/*": "./src/lib/*"
  }
}
```

To use a helper from `src/lib/utils.ts` in a route:

```ts
// src/lib/utils.ts
export function formatPrice(n: number) {
  return `$${n.toFixed(2)}`;
}
```

```svelte
<!-- src/routes/+page.svelte -->
<script lang="ts">
  import { formatPrice } from '#lib/utils.js';
</script>

<p>{formatPrice(9.5)}</p>
```

The same import works in `+page.ts`, `+page.server.ts` and `+server.ts`.

- **Extension:** the docs examples use `.js` (`#lib/utils.js`) even though the file is `utils.ts`. This is standard TypeScript ESM convention. Extensionless imports like `#lib/utils` are also resolved through the generated tsconfig paths.
- **Older projects:** if your project is on an older SvelteKit version, or `package.json` has no `imports` field, use `import { formatPrice } from '$lib/utils';`.
- **Keeping `$lib`:** you can bring it back by setting `kit.alias` to `{ '$lib': 'src/lib' }` in `svelte.config.js`.

Sources: the SvelteKit docs page for `#lib` and the merged PR "breaking: remove `$lib` in favor of `#lib`" (sveltejs/kit #16360).

Set `changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager` on the component:

```typescript
import { Component, ChangeDetectionStrategy } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-example',
  template: `{{ value }}`,
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager,
})
export class ExampleComponent {
  value = 0;
}
```

- **Eager and Default:** In the current docs, `Eager` is the new name for `ChangeDetectionStrategy.Default`. Angular checks an Eager component on every change detection run. User events, network responses and timers all trigger a run. `Default` still works, and the migration schematic rewrites it to `Eager`.
- **OnPush is the default from v22:** If you leave out `changeDetection`, the component is OnPush. To opt out, you have to set `Eager` explicitly. There's no global switch, so you set it per component.
- **Older versions:** Before v22, omitting `changeDetection` gave you the eager behavior. In those versions you can use `ChangeDetectionStrategy.Default` to opt out of OnPush.
- **Migration:** The `change-detection-eager` schematic adds `Eager` to components that don't set a strategy. It also converts `.Default` to `.Eager` and leaves `.OnPush` alone.

Set `changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager` on the component:

```typescript
import { Component, ChangeDetectionStrategy } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-my-widget',
  template: `{{ value }}`,
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager,
})
export class MyWidget {
  value = 0;
}
```

- In the newest Angular versions (v22 and later, according to the docs), `OnPush` is the default. A component with no `changeDetection` setting is therefore `OnPush`. To opt out, you must set `Eager` explicitly. There is no global setting for this.
- `ChangeDetectionStrategy.Default` is the older name for the same mode as `Eager`. On older versions, where `Default` is still the default, you can use `ChangeDetectionStrategy.Default` instead.
- In `Eager` mode, the component is checked whenever anything happens in the app, such as user events, network responses, or timers.
- The `change-detection-eager` schematic migration adds `Eager` to components that don't set a strategy. It also renames `.Default` to `.Eager`.

Set `changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager` on the component:

```ts
import { Component, ChangeDetectionStrategy } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-legacy-widget',
  template: `{{ state.value }}`,
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager, // checked on every CD pass
})
export class LegacyWidget {
  state = { value: 0 }; // mutated in place; Eager still picks it up
}
```

- **Angular v22 and later:** `OnPush` is now the default. A component with no `changeDetection` setting is `OnPush`, so you have to set `Eager` explicitly to opt out. The v22 migration adds `Eager` to existing components that had no explicit strategy, so they keep their old behavior.
- **`ChangeDetectionStrategy.Default`:** It's now a deprecated alias for `Eager` and is due to be removed. Older code that uses it still works, but use `Eager` for new code.
- **Before v22:** `Eager` was added as an alias in a minor release before v22. If your version doesn't have it, use `ChangeDetectionStrategy.Default`.

`Eager` makes Angular check the component whenever change detection reaches it, instead of only after an input change, an event in the component, `markForCheck`, or a changed signal.

An `OnPush` ancestor can still stop traversal from reaching an `Eager` child. Angular checks an `Eager` component only when change detection reaches it.

Sources: [ChangeDetectionStrategy API](https://angular.dev/api/core/ChangeDetectionStrategy), [Advanced component configuration](https://angular.dev/guide/components/advanced-configuration)

