Pass a `VertexAIBackend` instance in the options for `getAI()`. Both come from `firebase/ai`.

```js
import { initializeApp } from "firebase/app";
import { getAI, getGenerativeModel, VertexAIBackend } from "firebase/ai";

const app = initializeApp(firebaseConfig);

// Use the Vertex AI Gemini API backend (location is optional, defaults to "us-central1")
const ai = getAI(app, { backend: new VertexAIBackend("us-central1") });

const model = getGenerativeModel(ai, { model: "gemini-2.5-flash" });

const result = await model.generateContent("Write a haiku about Firebase.");
console.log(result.response.text());
```

- **Default backend:** `getAI(app)` with no options uses the Gemini Developer API. You get the same result by passing `new GoogleAIBackend()`. Passing `new VertexAIBackend()` is what switches providers.
- **Location:** `new VertexAIBackend(location)` takes an optional region string, and the default is `us-central1`. Only the Vertex AI backend supports setting a location. The Gemini Developer API doesn't.
- **Billing:** Using the Vertex AI Gemini API through Firebase AI Logic requires the pay-as-you-go Blaze plan. You also need to enable that API in your Firebase project.
- **Both providers:** You can use both in one app. Call `getAI` with a different backend for each, then create a model from each `ai` instance.
- **Naming:** Google has started calling Vertex AI the "Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform" in its docs. The JS reference I found still documents the class as `VertexAIBackend`, so use that name with 12.17.0. I didn't check the model name `gemini-2.5-flash` against the current models list, so confirm it there.
- **Don't use `getVertexAI`:** `getVertexAI` and the `firebase/vertexai` package are the older Vertex AI in Firebase API. Use `getAI` from `firebase/ai` instead.

Sources: [VertexAIBackend reference](https://firebase.google.com/docs/reference/js/ai.vertexaibackend) and the [`ai` package reference](https://firebase.google.com/docs/reference/js/ai).

Use the `backend` option of `getAI()` and pass a Vertex AI backend. That backend is now named `AgentPlatformBackend`.

```js
import { initializeApp } from "firebase/app";
import { getAI, getGenerativeModel, AgentPlatformBackend } from "firebase/ai";

const app = initializeApp(firebaseConfig);

// Use the Vertex AI Gemini API (now called the "Agent Platform Gemini API")
const ai = getAI(app, { backend: new AgentPlatformBackend("us-central1") });

const model = getGenerativeModel(ai, { model: "<model-name>" });
const result = await model.generateContent("Hello!");
console.log(result.response.text());
```

- **The rename:** Google Cloud renamed "Vertex AI" to "Agent Platform". A recent firebase-js-sdk change (PR #10184) added `AgentPlatformBackend` and deprecated `VertexAIBackend`. I couldn't confirm that 12.17.0 includes it. Check that `AgentPlatformBackend` is exported from `firebase/ai` in your install.
- **If it isn't exported:** Use `VertexAIBackend`, which works the same way:
  ```js
  import { getAI, VertexAIBackend } from "firebase/ai";
  const ai = getAI(app, { backend: new VertexAIBackend("us-central1") });
  ```
- **Default location:** `AgentPlatformBackend` defaults to `global`. `VertexAIBackend` defaults to `us-central1`. If your data residency depends on the region, pass the location explicitly.
- **Default backend:** `getAI(app)` without options uses the Gemini Developer API (`GoogleAIBackend`).
- **Billing:** This backend requires the Blaze (pay-as-you-go) plan. In the Firebase console, go to AI Logic and enable the Vertex AI / Agent Platform Gemini API provider.

Sources: [VertexAIBackend reference](https://firebase.google.com/docs/reference/js/ai.vertexaibackend), [PR #10184](https://github.com/firebase/firebase-js-sdk/pull/10184), [Firebase AI Logic FAQ](https://firebase.google.com/docs/ai-logic/faq-and-troubleshooting)

I can't verify the exact 1.7.0 API, so check this against the docs for your version. The approach below is the one from the 1.4+ line: the standalone `@better-auth/oauth-provider` plugin, which replaced the older `oidcProvider` plugin.

```bash
npm i @better-auth/oauth-provider
npx @better-auth/cli migrate   # adds the OAuth client/token/consent tables
```

**Server**
```ts
// auth.ts
import { betterAuth } from "better-auth";
import { jwt } from "better-auth/plugins";
import { oauthProvider } from "@better-auth/oauth-provider";

export const auth = betterAuth({
  disabledPaths: ["/token"], // the OAuth plugin provides /oauth2/token
  plugins: [
    jwt(), // signs ID tokens / JWT access tokens, exposes JWKS
    oauthProvider({
      loginPage: "/sign-in",   // where unauthenticated users are sent
      consentPage: "/consent", // your consent UI
    }),
  ],
});
```

**Client**
```ts
// auth-client.ts
import { createAuthClient } from "better-auth/client";
import { oauthProviderClient } from "@better-auth/oauth-provider/client";

export const authClient = createAuthClient({
  plugins: [oauthProviderClient()],
});
```

**Register a relying-party app**
```ts
const app = await authClient.oauth2.createClient({
  client_name: "Partner App",
  redirect_uris: ["https://partner.example.com/api/auth/callback/myapp"],
});
// -> returns client_id and client_secret
```

Other apps then use your server's discovery document (usually `/.well-known/openid-configuration`) to find the authorize, token, userinfo, and JWKS endpoints. Your `/consent` page should call the plugin's consent endpoint to approve or deny the request.

If you're on the older `oidcProvider` plugin from `better-auth/plugins`, it still works but is deprecated. Its setup is `oidcProvider({ loginPage: "/sign-in" })`, with the matching `oidcClient()` on the client.

