In current React Native, use `requestIdleCallback` and split the heavy work into small chunks. `InteractionManager` is deprecated. The docs say to avoid long-running work and use `requestIdleCallback` instead. The source also shows an invariant saying `InteractionManager` has been removed from core. The docs index has no 0.87.0 page, so this comes from the current docs.

```tsx
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { Text } from 'react-native';

function useChunkedWork<T>(items: T[], processItem: (item: T) => void) {
  const [done, setDone] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    let index = 0;
    let handle: number;
    let cancelled = false;

    const work = (deadline: IdleDeadline) => {
      // Process items only while the frame has idle time left
      while (index < items.length && deadline.timeRemaining() > 1) {
        processItem(items[index++]);
      }
      if (cancelled) return;
      if (index < items.length) {
        handle = requestIdleCallback(work); // continue next idle period
      } else {
        setDone(true);
      }
    };

    handle = requestIdleCallback(work);
    return () => {
      cancelled = true;
      cancelIdleCallback(handle);
    };
  }, [items, processItem]);

  return done;
}
```

- `requestIdleCallback` runs your callback when the JS thread is idle. Check `deadline.timeRemaining()` and yield when it runs low, so the work doesn't hold the thread.
- Break big tasks into small pieces. A single long synchronous function can't be interrupted, even inside an idle callback.
- Run animations on the native thread with `useNativeDriver: true`, or use Reanimated. They then stay smooth even when the JS thread is busy.
- Cancel the idle callback in the effect cleanup, as the example does.

In current React Native, `InteractionManager` is deprecated. The docs say to avoid long-running work and use the global `requestIdleCallback` instead. The source also shows dev builds throwing an error if you access `InteractionManager`. I couldn't find docs indexed for 0.87.0, so this follows the current docs.

Break the work into small chunks and run each chunk when the JS thread is idle. Use `timeRemaining()` so a chunk yields before it eats into a frame.

```tsx
import {useEffect, useState} from 'react';
import {Animated, Text} from 'react-native';

function processInChunks<T>(
  items: T[],
  work: (item: T) => void,
  onDone: () => void,
) {
  let i = 0;
  let id: number;

  const step = (deadline: IdleDeadline) => {
    // Do work only while the JS thread has idle time left
    while (i < items.length && deadline.timeRemaining() > 1) {
      work(items[i++]);
    }
    if (i < items.length) {
      id = requestIdleCallback(step); // continue in the next idle period
    } else {
      onDone();
    }
  };

  id = requestIdleCallback(step);
  return () => cancelIdleCallback(id); // cancel function
}

export function Screen({data}: {data: number[]}) {
  const [done, setDone] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    return processInChunks(data, item => heavyCompute(item), () => setDone(true));
  }, [data]);

  return <Text>{done ? 'Ready' : 'Working…'}</Text>;
}
```

A few tips:
- **Chunk the work.** `requestIdleCallback` can't interrupt a single long synchronous function. Split the work into small pieces.
- **Run animations on the UI thread.** Set `useNativeDriver: true` on `Animated` animations, or use Reanimated. They then keep running even when JS is busy.
- **Cancel on unmount.** Return `cancelIdleCallback(id)` from the effect cleanup, as the example does.

`InteractionManager` is gone in React Native 0.87. It was deprecated first, and the 0.87 release notes list it as removed. In `__DEV__`, accessing it now throws an error that points you to `requestIdleCallback`. The replacement is to split the work into small chunks and schedule them with the global `requestIdleCallback`. You don't import it.

```tsx
import {useEffect, useState} from 'react';
import {Text} from 'react-native';

function useProcessedItems<T, R>(items: T[], process: (item: T) => R) {
  const [results, setResults] = useState<R[]>([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    let index = 0;
    const out: R[] = [];
    let handle: number;

    const work = (deadline: IdleDeadline) => {
      // Process items only while the JS thread has idle time left
      while (index < items.length && deadline.timeRemaining() > 1) {
        out.push(process(items[index++]));
      }
      if (index < items.length) {
        handle = requestIdleCallback(work); // yield, then continue
      } else {
        setResults(out);
      }
    };

    handle = requestIdleCallback(work, {timeout: 2000}); // run eventually even if busy
    return () => cancelIdleCallback(handle); // cancel on unmount or dependency change
  }, [items, process]);

  return results;
}

export function List({items}: {items: number[]}) {
  const squares = useProcessedItems(items, n => n * n);
  return <Text>{squares.length} processed</Text>;
}
```

- **Chunk the work.** A callback that runs for a long time still blocks the single JS thread. Check `deadline.timeRemaining()` and reschedule the remainder.
- **Set a `timeout`.** Without it, a busy app might never call your callback.
- **Always cancel.** Call `cancelIdleCallback` in the effect cleanup.
- **Don't expect interaction awareness.** The old `runAfterInteractions` and interaction handles no longer do anything. The 0.86 stub behaved like `setImmediate`, which does not wait for animations. Idle callbacks only run when the JS thread is free.
- **Keep animations off the JS thread.** Use `useNativeDriver: true` with `Animated`, or Reanimated. Then JS work can't stall them at all.

The `requestIdleCallback` page in the React Native docs is still a stub that links to MDN, so MDN is the reference for its semantics.

```ts
import { Component, inject, signal } from '@angular/core';
import { HttpClient, HttpEventType } from '@angular/common/http';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-upload',
  template: `
    <input type="file" (change)="onFile($event)" />

    @if (progress() !== null) {
      <progress max="100" [value]="progress() ?? 0"></progress>
      {{ progress() }}%
    }
  `,
})
export class UploadComponent {
  private http = inject(HttpClient);
  progress = signal<number | null>(null);

  onFile(event: Event) {
    const file = (event.target as HTMLInputElement).files?.[0];
    if (!file) return;

    const body = new FormData();
    body.append('file', file);

    this.http
      .post('/api/upload', body, { reportProgress: true, observe: 'events' })
      .subscribe({
        next: (e) => {
          if (e.type === HttpEventType.UploadProgress && e.total) {
            this.progress.set(Math.round((100 * e.loaded) / e.total));
          } else if (e.type === HttpEventType.Response) {
            this.progress.set(100);
          }
        },
        error: () => this.progress.set(null),
      });
  }
}
```

The key parts are `reportProgress: true` and `observe: 'events'`, which make the request emit `HttpEventType.UploadProgress` events.

Make sure `provideHttpClient()` is in your app config.

If you enable `withFetch()`, upload progress won't work, because the Fetch API doesn't expose upload progress events. Use the default XHR backend for uploads.

I'm not aware of any changes to this API in v22, but check the v22 release notes if something behaves differently.

Set `reportProgress: true` and `observe: 'events'`, then handle `HttpEventType.UploadProgress`:

```ts
import { Component, inject, signal } from '@angular/core';
import { HttpClient, HttpEventType } from '@angular/common/http';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-upload',
  template: `
    <input type="file" (change)="onFileSelected($event)" />

    @if (uploading()) {
      <progress max="100" [value]="progress()"></progress>
      <span>{{ progress() }}%</span>
    }
  `,
})
export class UploadComponent {
  private http = inject(HttpClient);

  progress = signal(0);
  uploading = signal(false);

  onFileSelected(event: Event) {
    const file = (event.target as HTMLInputElement).files?.[0];
    if (!file) return;

    const formData = new FormData();
    formData.append('file', file);

    this.uploading.set(true);
    this.progress.set(0);

    this.http
      .post('/api/upload', formData, {
        reportProgress: true,
        observe: 'events',
      })
      .subscribe({
        next: (e) => {
          if (e.type === HttpEventType.UploadProgress && e.total) {
            this.progress.set(Math.round((100 * e.loaded) / e.total));
          } else if (e.type === HttpEventType.Response) {
            this.progress.set(100);
            this.uploading.set(false);
          }
        },
        error: () => this.uploading.set(false),
      });
  }
}
```

Make sure `provideHttpClient()` is in your app config:

```ts
export const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
  providers: [provideHttpClient()],
};
```

**Caveat:** if you use `provideHttpClient(withFetch())`, upload progress events are not emitted, because the Fetch API doesn't expose upload progress. For progress bars, use the default XHR backend (omit `withFetch()`).

I can't verify anything specific to Angular 22, but this API has been stable across recent versions.

`InteractionManager.runAfterInteractions` is the old answer, so don't use it. It has been deprecated since RN 0.8x, and its behavior was reduced to `setImmediate`, so it no longer waits for touches or animations. A PR to remove it entirely (#57026) was merged to `main` on June 1, 2026. I couldn't confirm whether that removal is in 0.87.0, but either way you should migrate off it. The RN docs now recommend splitting long tasks into small chunks and scheduling them with `requestIdleCallback`.

**1. Chunk heavy non-render work with `requestIdleCallback`**

```tsx
import {useEffect} from 'react';

function processInChunks<T>(
  items: T[],
  work: (item: T) => void,
  onDone?: () => void,
) {
  let i = 0;
  let handle: number;

  const run = (deadline: IdleDeadline) => {
    // Work only while the frame has idle time left (or the timeout fired)
    while (i < items.length && (deadline.timeRemaining() > 1 || deadline.didTimeout)) {
      work(items[i++]);
    }
    if (i < items.length) {
      handle = requestIdleCallback(run, {timeout: 500});
    } else {
      onDone?.();
    }
  };

  handle = requestIdleCallback(run, {timeout: 500});
  return () => cancelIdleCallback(handle); // call to cancel
}

// Usage in a component
useEffect(() => {
  const cancel = processInChunks(bigArray, indexItem);
  return cancel;
}, [bigArray]);
```

**2. Defer heavy rendering with concurrent React**

```tsx
import {useDeferredValue, useState} from 'react';
import {TextInput, View} from 'react-native';

function Search() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const deferredQuery = useDeferredValue(query); // lags behind, interruptible

  return (
    <View>
      <TextInput value={query} onChangeText={setQuery} />
      <ExpensiveList query={deferredQuery} />
    </View>
  );
}
```

You can also wrap a state update in `startTransition(() => setX(...))` from `useTransition`. Memoize `ExpensiveList` with `React.memo`, or the deferral gains you nothing.

**Caveats**
- `requestIdleCallback` only helps if each chunk is short. A single long synchronous function still blocks the JS thread.
- Keep animations off the JS thread. Use `useNativeDriver: true` or Reanimated, so JS work can't stall them.
- For truly CPU-heavy work, move it off the JS thread, for example to a native module or a worklet/background-thread library.

Sources: the RN InteractionManager docs (deprecated, points to `requestIdleCallback`), RN PR #57026, and the React docs for `useTransition`/`useDeferredValue`.

Upload progress needs two things: the XHR backend and `observe: 'events'` on the request.

**1. Use the XHR backend.** The default fetch backend doesn't support upload progress. It throws an error if you ask for it. Don't use `withXhr()` with SSR.

```ts
// app.config.ts
import { ApplicationConfig } from '@angular/core';
import { provideHttpClient, withXhr } from '@angular/common/http';

export const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
  providers: [provideHttpClient(withXhr())],
};
```

**2. Send the file and listen to the events.**

```ts
import { Component, inject, signal } from '@angular/core';
import { HttpClient, HttpEventType } from '@angular/common/http';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-upload',
  template: `
    <input type="file" (change)="onFileSelected($event)" />
    <progress max="100" [value]="progress()"></progress>
    {{ progress() }}%
  `,
})
export class UploadComponent {
  private http = inject(HttpClient);
  progress = signal(0);

  onFileSelected(event: Event) {
    const file = (event.target as HTMLInputElement).files?.[0];
    if (!file) return;

    const body = new FormData();
    body.append('file', file);

    this.http
      .post('/api/upload', body, {
        reportUploadProgress: true,
        observe: 'events',
      })
      .subscribe((e) => {
        if (e.type === HttpEventType.UploadProgress && e.total) {
          this.progress.set(Math.round((100 * e.loaded) / e.total));
        } else if (e.type === HttpEventType.Response) {
          this.progress.set(100);
        }
      });
  }
}
```

The docs I found disagree on the option name. The current HTTP guide uses `reportUploadProgress` and `reportDownloadProgress`. Another page and the older examples use `reportProgress: true`. I used `reportUploadProgress` because it's in the current guide. If your Angular 22 typings reject it, switch to `reportProgress: true`. The docs index didn't have 22.0.0 specifically, so these came from the current docs.

Upload progress needs two things. Switch the backend to XHR, because the default fetch backend doesn't support upload progress. Then request the raw event stream.

**1. Configure `HttpClient` with `withXhr()`**

```ts
// app.config.ts
import { ApplicationConfig } from '@angular/core';
import { provideHttpClient, withXhr } from '@angular/common/http';

export const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
  providers: [provideHttpClient(withXhr())],
};
```

Avoid `withXhr()` in SSR environments. The docs say the fetch backend is preferred there.

**2. Upload with `observe: 'events'` and show progress**

```ts
import { Component, inject, signal } from '@angular/core';
import { HttpClient, HttpEventType } from '@angular/common/http';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-upload',
  template: `
    <input type="file" (change)="onFile($event)" />
    @if (progress() !== null) {
      <progress max="100" [value]="progress()"></progress>
      <span>{{ progress() }}%</span>
    }
  `,
})
export class Upload {
  private http = inject(HttpClient);
  progress = signal<number | null>(null);

  onFile(event: Event) {
    const file = (event.target as HTMLInputElement).files?.[0];
    if (!file) return;

    const body = new FormData();
    body.append('file', file);

    this.http
      .post('/api/upload', body, {
        reportUploadProgress: true,
        observe: 'events',
      })
      .subscribe((e) => {
        switch (e.type) {
          case HttpEventType.UploadProgress:
            if (e.total) this.progress.set(Math.round((100 * e.loaded) / e.total));
            break;
          case HttpEventType.Response:
            this.progress.set(100);
            break;
        }
      });
  }
}
```

- The docs describe `reportUploadProgress` and `reportDownloadProgress` as the options that enable progress events. Some older examples and the `HttpRequest` API page still show `reportProgress: true`. If your type checks complain about one name, check which your installed version expects.
- Progress events are off by default because they have a performance cost.
- `e.total` can be undefined, so check it before dividing.
- With the default fetch backend, setting `reportUploadProgress` throws an error. That's why `withXhr()` is needed.

